children.push(p("Now you pull structured information out of the cleaned text: skills, education, job titles, years of experience. The right tool is Named Entity Recognition (NER). Start with a hybrid approach that is both accurate and easy to defend: a curated skill dictionary (a gazetteer of IT skills relevant to Nepal) combined with spaCy's NER. The dictionary guarantees you catch known skills; the NER model generalises to phrasing you did not anticipate. If time allows, fine-tune a custom spaCy NER model on your labelled resumes — but the hybrid baseline alone is a legitimate, publishable method."));

children.push(h2("Stage 4 — Turning text into numbers (representation)"));
children.push(p("A model cannot compare a resume to a job description as raw text; both must become numeric vectors. You have two levels here, and the contrast between them is one of the strongest points you can make in your defense."));
children.push(table(
  ["Method", "What it captures", "Use as"],
  [
    ["TF-IDF (scikit-learn)", "Keyword overlap and term importance. Fast, simple, fully explainable.", "Your baseline — proves the problem and gives a number to beat."],
    ["Sentence-BERT embeddings", "Meaning and context. Knows “TensorFlow” relates to “deep learning” even with no shared keyword.", "Your main model — solves the exact problem in your statement."],
  ],
  [2900, 4360, 2100]
));
children.push(spacer());
children.push(p("This directly answers the problem in your report — the candidate with a TensorFlow project being missed for a machine-learning role because the keyword did not match. TF-IDF would miss that candidate; Sentence-BERT would catch them because it understands the two concepts are semantically related. Showing the baseline failing and the semantic model succeeding on that exact example is a powerful result for your thesis."));

children.push(h2("Stage 5 — Matching and ranking"));
children.push(p("With both resumes and jobs as vectors, you compute how well each candidate fits each job. Use cosine similarity on the Sentence-BERT embeddings to produce a relevance score, then rank jobs for a candidate (and candidates for a job) by that score. To go further, add a supervised ranking model — Random Forest or Gradient Boosted Trees (XGBoost/LightGBM) — that combines the similarity score with structured features such as years of experience, education level, and skill-count overlap to produce a final, learned match score. This is the hybrid, content-based plus collaborative approach your report already describes."));
children.push(table(
  ["Task in the pipeline", "Model / technique", "Why it is the right choice"],
  [
    ["Parsing files to text", "pdfminer / python-docx", "Deterministic; ML is unnecessary and would add error."],
    ["Skill / entity extraction", "Skill dictionary + spaCy NER", "Combines guaranteed recall with generalisation; defensible baseline."],
    ["Text representation", "TF-IDF (baseline), Sentence-BERT (main)", "Baseline for comparison; semantic model solves the keyword problem."],
    ["Similarity scoring", "Cosine similarity", "Standard, interpretable measure of vector closeness."],
    ["Final match ranking", "Random Forest / Gradient Boosting", "Handles mixed features, robust, gives feature importance for explainability."],
  ],
  [2700, 2860, 3800]
));
children.push(spacer());

children.push(h2("Stage 6 — Evaluation"));
children.push(p("You must measure the system, not just build it. Report precision, recall, and F1-score for skill extraction and matching, and use ranking metrics — Mean Average Precision (MAP) and NDCG — to judge whether the right jobs appear near the top of each candidate's list. Compare your semantic model against the TF-IDF baseline so improvement is quantified. This evaluation section is often the difference between an average and a strong thesis grade."));

// 5. Why this works for Nepal
children.push(h1("5. Why This Stack Fits the Nepal Context"));
children.push(p("Your project's contribution is local relevance, so tie the technology back to it. Three choices carry that argument."));
children.push(bullet("**Hybrid skill extraction (dictionary + NER):** a Nepal-specific skill dictionary lets you encode local terminology and the real skills employers ask for, which global platforms ignore."));
children.push(bullet("**Semantic matching over keywords:** local resumes use varied wording for the same competency, exactly the case where Sentence-BERT beats keyword filters."));
children.push(bullet("**Lightweight, open-source stack:** every tool here is free and runs on modest hardware, which matters for a system built and demonstrated locally without cloud budgets."));

// 6. Recommended scope
children.push(h1("6. Recommended Scope — Build vs. Future Work"));
children.push(p("Protect your timeline by being explicit about the boundary between what you build and what you propose. A focused, working system beats an ambitious, half-finished one every time."));
children.push(table(
